  "dom_id": "step-q124392",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14229367/assets/e597e317f32a2a9a3757ce583a2b053f4cba1da1a2119c572671e7edc96c61e4.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{6}{7},\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ?$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14229367/assets/fb0da765a857607ce0c8f13690efaa2f0d504ccaa51899a23044ee4ed17f0aa5.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{6}{7}$ and $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{6}{7},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7})}{(\\frac{6}{7})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{6}.\n$$",
